//! English explanations of the NTP windows (G4.6b), scope `ntp`, sorted by key.

/// Explanations.
pub(super) const HELP: &[(&str, &str)] = &[
    ("ntp.clock", "## What it is\nWhat the client is doing to the system clock.\n\n- `slewing`: the difference is small (under 128 ms) and the clock runs slightly fast or slow until it is gone, with no jump.\n- `stepped`: the last answer set the clock at once.\n- `holding-spike`: a clock already right got a big difference; it stays as it is for up to 900 s, in case the answer is wrong (RFC 5905 §11.3).\n- `awaiting-confirmation`: the difference is over 1000 s and changes only when you confirm.\n- `refused`: the answer would put the clock before 2026 and is never applied.\n- `not-set`: only measuring: switched off in Set System Clock, or this backend or process cannot touch the clock (Clock Error says why)."),
    ("ntp.clock_error", "## What it is\nWhy the clock could not be changed. Usually the process lacks `CAP_SYS_TIME`, or this is the virtual lab, which never touches the clock of the computer it runs on."),
    ("ntp.dynamic_servers", "## What it is\nServers received from DHCP clients with `use_peer_ntp` (option 42), used after the configured ones. At most 8 in total."),
    ("ntp.enabled", "## What it does\nTurns the NTP client on.\n\n## Default\nOn in a new router (a config never saved), with no server configured: it asks only the servers the network's DHCP hands out (option 42) and nobody else. A saved config keeps what it says.\n\n## Careful\nWith Set System Clock on, it takes charge of the system clock."),
    ("ntp.freq_drift", "## What it is\nHow far this router's crystal is off, learned measurement after measurement. Positive: the crystal is slow. Back to zero when the service restarts, and learned again in a few polls."),
    ("ntp.last_adjustment", "## What it is\nThe size of the last correction, by slew or step."),
    ("ntp.last_error", "## What it is\nWhy the last poll failed, one line per server."),
    ("ntp.last_sync", "## What it is\nWhen the last good answer arrived."),
    ("ntp.max_poll", "## What it is\nLongest interval between polls, as a power of two in seconds: 10 = 17 min. The interval doubles with each good answer, up to this."),
    ("ntp.min_poll", "## What it is\nShortest interval between polls, as a power of two in seconds: 6 = 64 s. RFC 4330 §5 does not allow less."),
    ("ntp.next_poll", "## What it is\nWhen the next poll comes."),
    ("ntp.round_trip", "## What it is\nThe round trip to the server. The measurement's error is at most half of it (RFC 5905 §8)."),
    ("ntp.server.allowed", "## What it is\nWho may ask for the time, one item per line: a prefix (`192.168.0.0/16`), a range (`10.0.0.1-10.0.0.9`) or the name of a firewall **address list**, with its static and dynamic entries (IP › Firewall › Address Lists). Anyone else gets nothing, not even a refusal.\n\n## A list with no entries\nLets nobody in and shows under Empty Lists. A new entry counts within 2 s, with no change here.\n\n## Default\nLoopback, link-local and private networks (like VyOS), so a public WAN address never answers by accident. An empty list answers nobody.\n\n## Careful\nAllow public addresses only with a reason. Open NTP is a target for abuse (RFC 8633 §5.1)."),
    ("ntp.server.broadcast", "## What it does\nSends the time every 64 s to Broadcast Addresses (mode 5), without the client asking.\n\n## Careful\nNothing authenticates these messages: use it only on a trusted network (RFC 8633 §5.5). Off by default."),
    ("ntp.server.broadcast_addresses", "## What it is\nThe subnets' broadcast addresses (`192.168.88.255`), not a host's. IPv4 only."),
    ("ntp.server.denied", "## What it is\nQuestions from outside Allowed, ignored in silence."),
    ("ntp.server.empty_lists", "## What it is\nAddress lists named in Allowed that are empty or do not exist. They let nobody in: check the name."),
    ("ntp.server.enabled", "## What it does\nTurns the NTP server on (UDP port 123). Off by default.\n\n## Important\nIt answers only after the NTP client has synchronized, or with Use Local Clock. Until then it stays silent, so as not to spread a wrong time."),
    ("ntp.server.kisses", "## What it is\nKiss-o'-Death RATE notices sent to those who asked too much (RFC 5905 §7.4): at most one per 10 s per source."),
    ("ntp.server.last_error", "## What it is\nWhy the port did not open. Usually another service already uses UDP 123."),
    ("ntp.server.limited", "## What it is\nQuestions over the limit, after the notice, ignored in silence."),
    ("ntp.server.local_clock_stratum", "## What it is\nThe stratum announced for the local clock, 1 to 15.\n\n## Default\n5, as in RouterOS. Use a high value, so a client prefers any real source."),
    ("ntp.server.malformed", "## What it is\nDatagrams that are not a question (modes 6 and 7 included, RFC 9327 §6) or from an invalid source, ignored in silence."),
    ("ntp.server.rate_limit", "## What it does\nLimits each source to an average of one question per 2 s, with room for 16 (IPv6 counts by /64). Above that it sends a Kiss-o'-Death RATE (RFC 5905 §7.4), then stays silent.\n\n## When to turn it off\nOnly with many clients behind one address.\n\n## Default\nOn."),
    ("ntp.server.replies", "## What it is\nReplies sent."),
    ("ntp.server.requests", "## What it is\nWell-formed questions from allowed sources."),
    ("ntp.server.serving", "## What it is\nWhose time the server gives.\n\n- `upstream`: that of the server the client synchronized to. With no answers it goes on while the accumulated uncertainty stays under 1 s (about 18 h).\n- `local-clock`: this router's own clock (Use Local Clock).\n- `no`: nobody's; it stays silent."),
    ("ntp.server.stratum", "## What it is\nThe stratum announced: the upstream server's plus 1, or Local Clock Stratum."),
    ("ntp.server.unsynchronized", "## What it is\nQuestions received while the clock was not good, ignored in silence."),
    ("ntp.server.use_local_clock", "## What it does\nGives this router's own clock when there is no better source.\n\n## Careful\nThe local clock drifts with no correction: clients get a time that looks good and may be wrong. Use it on an isolated network with no NTP server in reach. The client shows `using-local-clock`."),
    ("ntp.server.vrf", "## What it is\nWhich VRF the server listens in. `main` is the main table."),
    ("ntp.servers", "## What it is\nOne per line: an IP address or a DNS name (`pool.ntp.org`). The client asks them all and keeps the answer with the smallest round trip.\n\n## When it may be empty\nWhen a DHCP client with `use_peer_ntp` brings the servers (Dynamic Servers). With neither the client waits, and the health check warns that the clock is not set."),
    ("ntp.set_system_clock", "## What it does\nApplies what is measured to the system clock. Under 128 ms by slewing, above by stepping; above 1000 s it asks for confirmation.\n\n## When to turn it off\nOn a general-purpose computer where chrony or timesyncd already own the clock: two owners fight. RouterOS has no such option.\n\n## Default\nOn. Needs the linux backend and `CAP_SYS_TIME`."),
    ("ntp.status", "## What it is\n`synchronized`: the last poll got a good answer. `waiting`: on, no good answer. `stopped`: off, or no daemon runs the client."),
    ("ntp.synced_server", "## What it is\nThe server of the measurement in use, and its address."),
    ("ntp.synced_stratum", "## What it is\nHow far the server is from a time source: 1 is attached to a reference clock, 2 asks a stratum 1, and so on."),
    ("ntp.system_offset", "## What it is\nThe server's time minus ours, in the last measurement. Positive: our clock is behind."),
    ("ntp.vrf", "## What it is\nWhich VRF reaches the servers. `main` is the main table. Outside it the socket is bound to the VRF (`SO_BINDTODEVICE`).\n\n## Limit\nDNS names are resolved by the router's resolver, which uses the main table."),
];
